  "assumptions": [
    "Illustration: a made-up two-setting loss bowl with simulated batch noise, not a real model trained on real data.",
    "Loss = (4x² + z²)/2, drawn as a surface at 0.4 × loss over x, z from −2 to 2. Left to right is 4 times steeper, so a learning rate above 0.5 overshoots and grows there; front to back it diverges only above 2.",
    "Step = −learning rate × (true slope + noise). The noise is Gaussian, centred on zero, with a spread of 2·√((64−B)/(63·B)) per direction: 2 at B=1 and exactly 0 at B=64.",
    "Arrows are drawn at true length (scale 1, bowl units): orange is the mini-batch step the ball takes next, grey is the full-data step, −learning rate × true slope.",
    "One host step is one update. A run stops at 40 updates (finished) or when the loss passes 10, the bowl's top (diverged). Noise comes from the fixed seed, so Reset replays the same noise.",
    "Each step lowers the loss only on average; with small batches a single step can raise it. Off-bowl positions are capped on screen; the Loss readout keeps the true value."
  ]
}